:"management service: one node that fronts the nodes behind it"

.comment("*****")
mgmt.kg runs on a management node: any node that can reach both the
operator's network and other nodes. It keeps a registry of those nodes,
checks that they are alive, relays calls to one of them or to all of
them, and stores source files on them. It is the role a baseboard
management controller has in a server; it is not tied to any board.

TRUST. Anyone who can reach the management node can run any code on it
and on every node behind it, and can read and replace the files of each
of them. Nothing is encrypted, and .srv.auth sees a user and a password
that travel in clear text. Let the management node listen on an isolated
or trusted network only.

Nodes are named by strings and reached by transport address strings,
the ones .cli takes; this file never looks inside an address.

mgmt.add(name;address)     register a node and try to connect to it; a
                           name that is there already gets the new address
mgmt.nodes                 the registry: a dictionary from name to
                           :{["addr" a] ["h" handle or 0] ["state" s]
                             ["seen" t] ["ver" v] ["wait" w] ["t" n]}
                           state is "up" or "down"; seen is the .pc()
                           time of the last answer, -1 before the first;
                           ver is what .ver() gave there; wait is 1
                           while a probe is unanswered; t is the node's
                           call timeout in seconds
mgmt.names()               the names, in the order they were added
mgmt.status()              name -> :{addr state seen age ver}: the
                           registry without the handles
mgmt.timeout(name;seconds) the call timeout of one node (mgmt.cfg.ct when
                           it was added)
mgmt.start(seconds)        check every node now and then every "seconds"
mgmt.stop()                stop checking
mgmt.open(name)            connect a node now (again, if it has a link)
mgmt.call(name;fn;args)    call fn on a node with the list of arguments
                           args and return the result. fn is a symbol
                           (the node's function of that name), a string
                           (source the node evaluates; with args [] its
                           value is the result) or a function (sent as
                           its source). An error on the node is raised
                           here as the same kind of error, with the
                           node's name before the message:
                             type error: n1: cannot apply + ...
                           A link that fails and a call that times out
                           are system errors that start with the name.
                           Errors of the management node itself start
                           with "mgmt: ".
mgmt.each(names;fn;args)   the same call on several nodes at once, and
mgmt.all(fn;args)          on all of them: a dictionary from name to the
                           result, or to an error value
                             :{["error" kind] ["node" name] ["message" m]}
                           The request goes out to every node before any
                           answer is waited for, so the nodes work at
                           the same time, and each node has until its
                           own timeout: a node that is dead or slow
                           costs at most its timeout and never loses the
                           others' results. A node without a link is an
                           error value at once while the check runs (the
                           check connects it); with no check running it
                           is connected first, after the others have
                           their requests.
mgmt.put(name;path;text)   write a file on a node; the number of
                           characters written
mgmt.boot(name;text)       write the node's boot.kg, which it runs when
                           it starts
mgmt.load(name;path)       have the node load a file now: .l(path) there
mgmt.reset(name)           node.reset(name), when this board has one
mgmt.power(name;on)        node.power(name;on), when this board has one

The file is a module: mgmt::.import("mgmt"), and every name below is an entry of
the dictionary mgmt (mgmt.call, mgmt.add, ...). The helpers and the state
(the timer, the request counter) are private to the module.

Settings, in the dictionary mgmt.cfg (written as mgmt.cfg.ct::60): mgmt.cfg.pi, the
interval mgmt.start was given; mgmt.cfg.ct, the call
timeout a node gets when it is added (30 seconds; this and the interval
of 5 seconds in the example are provisional); mgmt.cfg.probe, the request a
check sends (".ver()").

Liveness. Every interval each node is sent mgmt.cfg.probe without waiting.
An answer makes the node "up". A node that has not answered by the next
check is "down"; so is one whose link failed or whose call timed out,
and that one is connected again at the following check. A node that is
busy for longer than the interval is therefore down until it answers.
Connecting waits for the node: one that accepts a connection and then
says nothing holds the check for the transport's handshake time.

While mgmt.call or mgmt.each waits for nodes, the management node does
nothing else: no check runs and other clients wait.

On a node, connecting defines three names: .mgmt.run, .mgmt.job and
.mgmt.r (names that start with a dot: one name each, in the node's globals). Nothing else is installed there and nothing is kept on storage.

Example configuration (boot.kg of a management node; the addresses are
this example's, nothing in the library depends on them):

    mgmt::.import("mgmt")
    mgmt.add("a";"tcp:10.0.0.11:5000")
    mgmt.add("b";"uart:1@921600")
    mgmt.add("c";"uart:2@921600")
    mgmt.start(5)
    .srv(5000)

From a desktop (KlongPy, or any node; the node has imported mgmt in its boot.kg
as above, and a request names an entry of the dictionary):

    mgmt::.cli("10.0.0.10:5000")
    mgmt(:mgmt.call,"a",:sq,,[7])            --> 49
    mgmt(:mgmt.all,"{+/x}",,,!100)           --> :{["a" 4950] ...}
    mgmt(:mgmt.boot,"a",,"sq::{x*x}")
    mgmt("mgmt.status()")

Runtimes. The native runtime has .try and .err, which this file uses to
catch an error and to raise one, on the management node and on the
nodes. KlongPy has neither. As a management node it loads this file and
works with these differences (mgmt.cfg.trap is 0 there): mgmt.add does not
connect, the first call does; an error of a node passes through
mgmt.call with its kind but without the node's name; mgmt.each fails as
a whole when a link fails or a node is too slow (an error on a node is
still an error value); the check marks a node down but does not connect
it again, because .cli blocks for minutes there when nobody listens
(mgmt.open(name) does it); an error of the management node itself is a
name error about mgmt.error, after the message is printed. At strict
level 2 KlongPy cannot be asked whether .try exists: set mgmt.trap::0
after loading. The nodes are native runtimes in every case.
*****

nodes:::{}
cfg:::{;"pi";5;"ct";30;"probe";".ver()";"trap";-1}
thandle::0
seqno::0
jobreq::0

:"----- what a node runs; sent to it as source when it is connected -----"

:"mgmt.run(fn;args): the call, with its error as a value: ["" result] or [0 kind message]"
:"(a string before the result, not a number: a list of two numbers would travel as one vector,"
:"and the result would take the other's type)"
.mgmt.run::{[r];r::.try({[f];f:::[~@x;.E(x):|x~*.E("[",($x),"]");.E(1_$x);x];:[(0=#y)&~@x;f;f@y]};(,x),,y);
           :[r@0;(,""),,r@1;r]}
:"mgmt.job(id;fn;args): the same, kept for the collection that follows"
.mgmt.job::{.mgmt.r::x,,.mgmt.run(y;z);0}
.mgmt.r::0
:"mgmt.wr(path;text): write a file"
wr::{[c];c::.oc(x);.tc(c);.d(y);.tc(0);.cc(c);#y}

:"----- errors -----"

cap::{:[cfg.trap<0;cfg.trap::.E("~.try~*[:.try]");0];1~cfg.trap}
:"mgmt.try(f;args): [1 result] or [0 kind message]; without .try the error is not caught"
trycall::{:[cap();.try(x;y);(,1),,x@y]}
fail::{:[cap();.err(x;"mgmt: ",y);nofail("mgmt: ",y)]}
nofail::{.p(x);mgmterror(x)}
ev::{[d];d:::{};d,"error",,x;d,"node",,y;d,"message",,z;d}

:"----- registry -----"

nodeof::{[e];e::nodes?x;:[:undefined~e;fail("name";"no such node: ",$x);e]}
names::{{x@0}'nodes}
add::{[e];:[:undefined~nodes?x;0;lost(x)];e:::{};e,"addr",,y;e,"h",,0;e,"state",,"down";e,"seen",,-1;e,"ver",,0;e,"wait",,0;e,"t",,cfg.ct;
           nodes,x,,e;:[cap();trycall(open;,x);0];x}
timeout::{(nodeof(x)),"t",,y;y}
st1::{[e d s];e::x@1;s::e?"seen";d:::{};d,"addr",,e?"addr";d,"state",,e?"state";d,"seen",,s;
           d,"age",,:[s<0;-1;.pc()-s];d,"ver",,e?"ver";(x@0),,d}
mk::{[d];d:::{};{x,y}/(,d),x}
status::{mk(st1'nodes)}

:"----- links -----"

:"mgmt.open(name): connect and give the node its three names; the handle, or an error"
open::{[e h];e::nodeof(x);lost(x);h::.cli(e?"addr");.clit(h;e?"t");e,"ver",,h(cfg.probe);
            h([:set :.mgmt.r 0]);h([:set :.mgmt.run],,.mgmt.run);h([:set :.mgmt.job],,.mgmt.job);
            e,"h",,h;e,"state",,"up";e,"seen",,.pc();h}
:"mgmt.lost(name): the link is gone"
lost::{[e h];e::nodes?x;h::e?"h";e,"h",,0;e,"state",,"down";e,"wait",,0;:[h~0;0;.clic(h)]}
seen::{x,"state",,"up";x,"seen",,.pc();1}
:"mgmt.h(name): the handle, connecting when there is none; errors carry the node's name"
handle::{[e h];e::nodeof(x);h::e?"h";:[~h~0;h:|~cap();open(x);handle2(x;trycall(open;,x))]}
handle2::{:[y@0;y@1;.err(y@1;x,": ",y@2)]}

:"----- liveness -----"

nop::{x}
pong::{[e];e::nodes?x;:[:undefined~e;0;pong2(e;y)]}
pong2::{x,"wait",,0;x,"ver",,y;seen(x)}
:"mgmt.ping(name): send the probe. The entry says down and has no handle while this"
:"runs, so that an error here leaves the node to be connected again."
ping::{[e h s a];e::nodes?x;h::e?"h";s::e?"state";e,"h",,0;e,"state",,"down";
            .clit(h;e?"t");a::.async(h;pong(x;));a(cfg.probe);
            e,"h",,h;e,"state",,s;e,"wait",,1;1}
chk::{[e h];e::nodes?x;h::e?"h";:[1~e?"wait";e,"state",,"down";0];
           :[h~0;:[cap();open(x);0];ping(x)];1}
chk1::{[r];r::trycall(chk;,x);:[r@0;1;lost(x)]}
chk2::{[a];a::.async(chk1;nop);a(x)}
tick::{chk2'names();1}
tock::{tick()}
stop::{[t];t::thandle;thandle::0;:[t~0;0;.timerc(t)]}
start::{stop();cfg.pi::x;tick();thandle::.timer("mgmt";x;tock);1}

:"----- relay -----"

req::{:[(0=#y)&~@x;x;(,x),y]}
call::{:[cap();callt(x;y;z);calld(x;y;z)]}
calld::{[e h r];e::nodeof(x);h::handle(x);.clit(h;e?"t");r::h(req(y;z));seen(e);r}
callt::{[e h r];e::nodeof(x);h::handle(x);.clit(h;e?"t");r::.try(h;,[:.mgmt.run],(,y),,z);
             :[r@0;res(x;e;r@1);gone(x;r)]}
res::{seen(y);:[0~z@0;.err(z@1;x,": ",z@2);z@1]}
gone::{lost(x);.err(y@1;x,": ",y@2)}

:"----- fan-out -----"

lose::{lost(x);ev(y@1;x;x,": ",y@2)}
:"issue: 1 sent, 0 not connected, or an error value"
snd::{[e h a];e::nodes?x;h::e?"h";.clit(h;e?"t");a::.async(h;nop);a(jobreq);1}
iss::{[r];r::trycall(snd;,x);:[r@0;1;lose(x;r)]}
is1::{[e];e::nodes?x;:[:undefined~e;ev("name";"mgmt";"mgmt: no such node: ",$x):|0~e?"h";0;iss(x)]}
isc::{[r];r::trycall(open;,x);:[r@0;iss(x);ev(r@1;x;x,": ",r@2)]}
:"a node without a link is connected here only when no check is running to do it"
is2::{:[~0~y;y:|cap()&thandle~0;isc(x);ev("system";x;x,": not connected")]}
:"collect"
got::{:[@z;ev("system";x;x,": no result");got2(x;z@0;z@1)]}
got2::{:[~seqno~y;ev("system";x;x,": no result"):|0~z@0;ev(z@1;x;x,": ",z@2);z@1]}
get::{[e h r];e::nodes?x;h::e?"h";
           :[h~0;ev("system";x;x,": not connected");get2(x;e;trycall(h;[:.mgmt.r]))]}
get2::{:[z@0;get3(x;y;z@1);lose(x;z)]}
get3::{seen(y);got(x;y;z)}
col::{:[1~y;get(x);y]}
each::{[s];seqno::seqno+1;jobreq::[:.mgmt.job],seqno,(,y),,z;
            s::is1'x;s::x is2's;s::x col's;mk(x{x,,y}'s)}
all::{each(names();x;y)}

:"----- deploy -----"

put::{call(x;wr;(,y),,z)}
boot::{put(x;"boot.kg";y)}
load::{call(x;".l(""",y,""")";!0)}

:"----- hooks a board may provide -----"

hook::{[d f];d::.E("node");f:::[@d;:undefined;d?x];:[:_f;fail("name";"this board has no node.",x);f]}
reset::{[f];nodeof(x);f::hook("reset");f(x)}
power::{[f];nodeof(x);f::hook("power");f(x;y)}

.export(:{;"add";add;"nodes";nodes;"cfg";cfg;"names";names;"status";status;"timeout";timeout;"start";start;"stop";stop;
  "open";open;"call";call;"each";each;"all";all;"put";put;"boot";boot;"load";load;"reset";reset;"power";power})
